# Licensing

Copyright the Korpis contributors.

Korpis is not licensed under a single licence. It is **split by layer**, deliberately, for the
reasons argued in §5 of [`00-overview.md`](./00-overview.md) and restated in §2 of
[`19-governance.md`](./19-governance.md).

| Layer | Licence | Full text |
|---|---|---|
| Protocol definitions, client SDKs, recipe format, agent protocol | **Apache-2.0** | [`LICENSE-APACHE-2.0.txt`](./LICENSE-APACHE-2.0.txt) |
| Control plane, node agent, web / CLI / chat clients | **AGPL-3.0-or-later** | [`LICENSE-AGPL-3.0.txt`](./LICENSE-AGPL-3.0.txt) |
| Recipes, extensions, themes | Author's choice, proprietary included | — |

**Why the split.** Anything a third party must implement to interoperate — the wire protocol, the
recipe format, the agent contract — is Apache-2.0, because Rule K-7 requires that someone else be
able to write a conforming agent without friction, and a copyleft protocol definition would defeat
that. Apache-2.0 also carries an explicit patent grant. The platform itself is AGPL, because running
it for other people is exactly the case AGPL exists to cover.

Extensions are separate processes speaking a published contract, not derivative works. They may be
licensed however their authors wish, including proprietary and paid.

## What this means if you run it

Stated plainly, because AGPL is routinely misread as a restriction on use:

**Run it commercially. Sell capacity on it. Charge whatever you like. Build closed extensions
against it. You owe nothing.**

The obligation arises in one case only: if you **modify Korpis itself** and offer the modified
version to others over a network, you publish those modifications. Using it unmodified obliges
nothing at all. There is no per-workload, per-node, per-instance, per-account or per-domain charge,
at any scale, ever.

## Contributions

Contributions are accepted under the **Developer Certificate of Origin** — sign off with
`git commit -s`. There is no contributor licence agreement and no copyright assignment, which keeps
copyright distributed and makes a future relicence require broad consent from contributors. That is
the point: it forecloses an open-core pivot structurally rather than by promise. See
[`CONTRIBUTING.md`](./CONTRIBUTING.md).

## Documentation

The documents in this repository are the specification. Until the first code release they are the
whole of the work, and they are published under **CC BY 4.0** so that they can be quoted, argued
with, and built on freely. Attribution is the only condition.

## Trademark

The Korpis name and marks are held separately from the code and are not licensed by the above.
Unmodified builds may use them freely, and **commercial hosting under the Korpis name is explicitly
permitted**. A modified distribution must be renamed — not to discourage forks, but so that a user
who downloads something called Korpis gets Korpis and security advisories refer to a knowable
artifact. See §3 of [`19-governance.md`](./19-governance.md).
